  "correct": "B",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find the parallel tangent",
      "body": "<p>Translate the line upwards, keeping gradient 2, until it just touches the upward parabola. At contact the curve has gradient 2. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, find this contact point.</p>",
      "recap": "The closest point of a convex parabola to a disjoint line lies where its tangent is parallel to that line.",
      "pitfall": "The distance sought is perpendicular to the line, not the vertical gap at the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and substitute into the curve.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l03",
          "reminder": "The closest point of a convex parabola to a disjoint line lies where its tangent is parallel to that line."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Draw the perpendicular through the contact point",
      "body": "<p>The contact point is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. A line perpendicular to gradient 2 has gradient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so its equation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Perpendicular nonvertical lines have gradients whose product is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.",
      "pitfall": "The perpendicular passes through the point on the curve, not through the origin.",
      "pause": "Find where this perpendicular meets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l03",
          "reminder": "Perpendicular nonvertical lines have gradients whose product is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the distance between the two points",
      "body": "<p>Solve the perpendicular-line equation together with the given line to locate the foot of the perpendicular. Use its coordinate differences from the contact point to calculate the distance.</p>",
      "recap": "Pythagoras gives the straight-line distance from horizontal and vertical coordinate differences.",
      "pitfall": "Do not stop at the squared distance.",
      "pause": "Find the intersection coordinates and apply Pythagoras to the resulting segment.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l01",
          "reminder": "Pythagoras gives the straight-line distance from horizontal and vertical coordinate differences."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>The parallel tangent has gradient 2, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> gives the curve point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its tangent is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Indeed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the entire parabola lies on the far side of this parallel tangent from the given line.</p><p>The shortest connecting segment is perpendicular to both parallel lines. Through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> it has equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Combining this with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p>The distance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>: <strong>B.</strong></p>",
